fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1110", description: some(value: "A FORWARDING ROW IS OFFERED AS A PROVIDER CANDIDATE, AND IT IS NOT A PROVIDER. Found while writing library proposal 007 (WI-1109), and the finding corrected two of my own claims.\n\nTHE DEFECT. `Ord provides WeakOrd[T = T]` is a spec forwarding — a rule about the relation, not a claim that anything has type `Ord`. But `impl_sorts_providing_spec(WeakOrd)` returns `Ord`, so the resolver offers it as a candidate provider, and `Ord requires WeakOrd[T]` then closes a loop. MEASURED: a carrier providing `Eq` and `PartialOrd` but no `compare` gets\n\n  type mismatch in WeakOrd.compare.dispatch: ... unresolved: WeakOrd[T = Half]\n  (construction is cyclic: WeakOrd[T = Half] -> WeakOrd[T = Half])\n\nwhere the truthful answer is 'no impl provides WeakOrd'.\n\nIT IS A DIAGNOSTIC WART, NOT A WRONG ANSWER — the cycle detector catches it, so the program is refused either way. That is why this is a ticket and not a hotfix.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS IT FORCED, both recorded at their sites. (1) WI-1109's first probe of this used a carrier with NO provisions at all, which fails at `PartialOrd` before the cycle can form, and I read that clean refusal as the cycle being ABSENT from the ordering tower. It is not absent, it is MASKED by an earlier failing requirement — the probe was too weak to see it. (2) eq.anthill's note claimed the cycle was why `Eq` cannot forward to `PartialEq`. Also wrong: both towers cycle identically. The real reason `Eq` keeps `requires` is NAMING — dropping it gives 32 failures, every one `unresolved import 'anthill.prelude.Eq.eq'`, because proposal 004 kept `Eq.eq` resolving through the requires-chain (WI-614) for source compatibility and `provides` puts nothing in a spec's own scope.\n\nTHE FIX, and why it needs its own measurement rather than being obvious: exclude IDENTITY-FORWARDING rows from candidate collection. `is_identity_forwarding` (kb/typing.rs) already names the predicate, so the change is small. The risk is that `Stream provides Iterable` (stream.anthill) is exactly the same shape and has been in the library far longer — value-directed dispatch of `Iterable.find`/`map` on a stream may reach `Stream` as a candidate deliberately, and WI-495/WI-496 DELETED List's explicit `provides Iterable` on the strength of that route. So the acceptance must show Iterable dispatch on List/Relation/MappedStream unchanged, not merely that the cycle is gone.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the Half fixture above reports 'no impl provides WeakOrd' rather than a cycle, driven with a value-carrying control that a real provider still resolves; wi496_transitive_iterator_test, wi495_non_stream_iterable_test and wi1076_self_representing_spec_carrier_test stay green; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/library/007-weak-vs-strong-ordering.md §Open questions 3."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-16T01:26:02Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1110", author: "user", content: "MECHANISM INSTRUMENTED, AND THE TICKET'S EARLIER TEXT CORRECTED 2026-08-15. I had twice asserted the mechanism from a symptom; a user pushed back and this is what `collect_provides_candidates` actually returns, printed on the SHIPPED tree with no experiment applied.\n\nCONFIRMED — the forwarding row IS returned as a candidate, at every binding:\n\n  WeakOrd[T = Int64]           -> [Ord, Int64]\n  WeakOrd[T = String]          -> [Ord, String]\n  WeakOrd[T = Var(Rigid ...)]  -> [Ord]            <- the ONLY candidate\n\nSo `Ord` answers goals it cannot possibly satisfy — nothing has type `Ord` — and at an ABSTRACT element it is the only answer offered. That is the ticket's core claim and it holds.\n\nCORRECTED — the vacuity is NOT reachable on the shipped ordering tower. The same probe's program (a body dispatching `WeakOrd.compare` at an abstract T with NO requires, the wi325 shape one tower over) is REFUSED. Earlier feedback on this ticket said the `requires` clause was 'accidentally' preventing vacuity by inference from the Eq experiment; that inference is now MEASURED and correct, but it means there is no wrong answer on main today. The Eq experiment went vacuous because `provides` ALONE leaves no cycle to reject the candidate.\n\nSHARPER THAN 'A DIAGNOSTIC WART', which is how this was filed: every WeakOrd goal at a real carrier carries a SPURIOUS SECOND CANDIDATE (`[Ord, Int64]` where only `Int64` can answer), eliminated only by cycle detection running and failing. Ordering resolution is therefore doing avoidable work on every goal, and correctness rests on a rejection rather than on the candidate never being offered. That is the argument for the fix, and it is stronger than the message-quality one the ticket opened with.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG AND WHY IT MATTERS FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS: I claimed 'the row matches any T' from behaviour, then built a probe using a carrier I called provision-less. It was not — `enum Bare { entity b(v: Int64) }` is a TOTAL composite and `eq_derive` asserts `provides PartialEq` + `provides Eq` for it (WI-664), so the probe measured nothing and its own control (which also loaded) should have told me so immediately. Do not test 'a carrier with no equality' with a composite; there isn't one.", at: "2026-08-15T22:30:06Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1110", author: "user", content: "THE DESCRIPTION ABOVE IS SUPERSEDED. It says the fix is \"exclude identity-forwarding rows from candidate collection\" — filtering a symptom. The right fix is one level up and was supplied by the user (2026-08-15): A CHAIN ENTRY CARRIES A SUPPLY SOURCE.\n\nTHE MODEL.\n\n  requires A[T]   the A dictionary is PASSED IN  — an inbound slot the caller fills.\n  provides A[T]   the A dictionary is CONSTRUCTED FROM SELF — we already hold what is\n                  needed and can supply it.\n\nBoth are entries in the dictionary chain. They differ ONLY in where the dictionary comes from. `B provides A[T]` reads as \"we know how to obtain an A from a B\" — a conversion, which is what B <: A means operationally, and which is why writing both clauses (as WI-1109 shipped) is one edge represented twice.\n\nTHE CATEGORY ERROR IT NAMES, and every symptom follows from it. A CARRIER's `provides` is a FACT ABOUT THE WORLD (\"Int64 is an Ord\") and belongs in a searchable provider table. A SPEC's `provides` is a CONVERSION (\"from an Ord dictionary you can make a WeakOrd one\") and belongs in the chain. WI-1109 put a conversion into the provider table.\n\n  * `Ord` returned as a candidate for EVERY WeakOrd goal (instrumented: WeakOrd[T=Int64]\n    -> [Ord, Int64]; at a rigid var -> [Ord] alone) — a conversion sitting in the\n    provider search space.\n  * Needing `Ord requires WeakOrd` AS WELL — the conversion could not supply a\n    dictionary, so an inbound slot was added to get one.\n  * The cycle — the same edge as both a provider and a requirement.\n  * `Eq provides PartialEq` going VACUOUS with `provides` alone (measured: wi325's\n    missing-requires refusal flipped to a clean load) — a universal provider row with no\n    cycle left to reject it.\n  * `Eq.eq` ceasing to resolve under `provides` alone (measured: 32 failures, all\n    `unresolved import 'anthill.prelude.Eq.eq'`) — naming follows the CHAIN (WI-614), and\n    `provides` never put PartialEq there.\n\nWHAT IT BUYS. (1) ONE CLAUSE where WI-1109 needs two, which is what was asked for\noriginally. (2) The Eq tower becomes forwardable after all: a self-supplied chain entry\nputs PartialEq IN THE CHAIN, so `Eq.eq` keeps resolving AND carriers stop writing the\nlower floor — the two options recorded as mutually exclusive in eq.anthill stop being so,\nand that note must be rewritten when this lands. (3) The spurious second candidate on\nevery ordering goal goes away, so correctness stops resting on cycle detection rejecting\na candidate that should never have been offered.\n\nWHERE IT GOES, and it is not new machinery: `ProviderDictChain.entries` ALREADY mixes two\nentry kinds — sort-level `requires` then the provisions' conditions — with a parallel\n`conditions_for` array marking each slot's provenance and strictness. A third provenance\nis the natural extension of that pattern, not a new one.\n\nCOSTS, both real. Dictionary ARITY moves: wi857's `the_layout_counts_what_resolve_bundles`\ncounts slots and already went 2 -> 3 in WI-1109; it will move again, and the count IS the\nassertion there. And the loader must begin DISTINGUISHING a carrier's `provides` from a\nspec's, which it does not do today — `is_identity_forwarding` (kb/typing.rs) is the\nnearest existing predicate and is the wrong shape for it (it tests the BINDINGS, not\nwhether the subject is a carrier).\n\nONE PREDICTION TO DRIVE RATHER THAN ASSUME. That NAMING follows a self-supplied entry the\nway it follows a `requires` one. That is the claim doing all the work for `Eq.eq`, and\nthis ticket must measure it before relying on it — three times in the WI-1109 arc a\nmechanism was asserted from a symptom and was wrong (the row \"matching any T\", the cycle\nbeing peculiar to equality, and a probe whose \"provision-less\" carrier was a Total\ncomposite that eq_derive had already given both provisions).\n\nSUPERSEDES PART OF WI-1109. `Ord requires WeakOrd[T]` + `provides WeakOrd[T = T]` becomes\n`provides WeakOrd[T = T]` alone. ordered.anthill's comment claiming the two clauses \"are\nnot redundant and not circular\" is correct ONLY under the current model and must be\nrewritten, not preserved: under this one they ARE the same edge, and that is the point.\n\nACCEPTANCE.\n * `Ord provides WeakOrd[T = T]` with NO `requires WeakOrd`, and an `Ord`-constrained body\n   still reaches `compare` — driven, value asserted (the wi1109 `an_ord_constrained_body_\n   reaches_compare` shape, which currently pins the OPPOSITE arrangement and must flip).\n * `collect_provides_candidates` for a `WeakOrd` goal returns ONLY real carriers —\n   instrumented in this ticket's own test, since the defect is invisible from the outside\n   while cycle detection hides it.\n * A body dispatching a `WeakOrd` spec op with NO requires in scope is still REFUSED, with\n   the control that the same body WITH the requires loads and runs.\n * `Eq provides PartialEq[T = T]` either lands (with `Eq.eq` still resolving and wi325's\n   refusal still firing) or is refused with a measured reason recorded at eq.anthill —\n   whichever, the note there stops saying the two clauses are mutually exclusive.\n * wi857's dictionary-layout counts updated to whatever the new layout is, deliberately.\n * Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-08-15T22:38:03Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1110", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The supply-source model is implemented, and it collapsed FOUR clauses to one on the ordering tower and made the equality tower forwardable after all.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED, three wirings that are separable and were measured separately.\n\n 1. A SPEC'S `provides` IS A CHAIN ENTRY (`self_supplied_entries`, kb/typing.rs). `RequiresEntry` gained `supply: SupplySource` — `Required` (the caller fills it) or `SelfSupplied` (the provider builds it from itself) — set at the one producer and read at the two sites that must agree with it.\n 2. A CONVERSION IS NOT A PROVIDER (`collect_provides_candidates` skip). The row stays a fact, so `derive_forwarded_provisions` still materializes each carrier's own row; it just stops being offered as an answer to a goal nothing of that type can answer.\n 3. A CONVERSION LENDS ITS NAMES (scan sub-pass 2, kb/load.rs). `requires` already wired the target's scope as a parent; `provides` now does too, which is what keeps `import anthill.prelude.Ord.{gte}` and `Eq.{eq}` resolving with no `requires` left in either spec.\n\n`forwarded_to_requires` (WI-1109's escape in `check_provider_requires`) is DELETED and replaced by \"a conversion owes nothing here\" — the obligation is relocated to the carrier through the chain instead of to the forwarder's own `requires`, which is why `Ord` no longer restates `requires Eq[T]` / `requires PartialOrd[T]`.\n\nSTDLIB. `Ord` went from four clauses to one:\n  requires Eq[T] + requires PartialOrd[T] + requires WeakOrd[T] + provides WeakOrd[T = T]\n    ->  provides WeakOrd[T = T]\n`Eq requires PartialEq[T]` -> `Eq provides PartialEq[T = T]`, so a carrier writing `provides Eq[X]` gets `provides PartialEq[X]` DERIVED. Two wi224 fixtures that were pinned to fail with `provides 'Eq', which requires 'PartialEq'` now load clean, which is the benefit rather than a loosening.\n\nTHE PREDICATE NEEDED TWO QUESTIONS, AND THE FIRST CUT ONLY ASKED ONE. \"Does the row bind the target's carrier parameter to one of the subject's own type parameters\" is the SHAPE of a conversion — and a PARAMETRIC WITNESS has it too. `sort AnyM { sort E = ?  provides Monoid[T = E]  operation combine(a: E, b: E) = 99 }` (wi841) binds Monoid's carrier to its own parameter exactly as `Ord` binds WeakOrd's, and IS a Monoid dictionary because it carries `combine`. MEASURED: with the shape test alone, AnyM's own `Monoid` slot resolved by search back to AnyM and all three wi841 selection tests failed `construction is cyclic: Monoid[T = Int64] -> Monoid[T = Int64]`. So the predicate also asks whether the subject supplies any of the target's operations. `a_parametric_witness_is_still_a_candidate` is the control.\n\nAND AN EARLIER CUT KEYED ON `is_identity_forwarding` — BINDINGS ALONE — WHICH SWEPT IN THE STREAM FAMILY. `LogicalStream provides Stream[T = T, E = E]`, `FiniteStream provides Stream[T, E]` and `Relation provides LogicalStream[T = T, E = E]` all have identity bindings and are MEMBERSHIP claims: those specs are SELF-REPRESENTING, they are their own carriers, and value-directed dispatch reaches `Stream.splitFirst` on a `Relation` through them. Reading them as conversions put a slot in three dictionaries and broke six tests across wi210/wi224/wi411/wi474, element threading included. `spec_carrier_param_or_sole` returning `None` for a self-representing spec is what excludes them, and it is the same owner WI-1076 established — not a new predicate.\n\nTHE PREDICATE THE DERIVATION KEYS ON IS DELIBERATELY WIDER. `forwarded_rows_to_derive` still uses `is_identity_forwarding`; `provision_is_conversion` is a strict subset of it. The direction that has to hold is `conversion => derived-through` — a row excluded from the search must have its carriers' rows materialized, or the exclusion deletes an answer instead of relocating it. The converse is false on purpose: a Stream-family row keeps BOTH its derived rows and its search role. Stated at both sites.\n\nMEASURED BACK-OUTS, and the first one REFUTED my own prediction.\n * remove the candidate skip -> 1876 of 2858 fail, stdlib included, `construction is cyclic: PartialEq[X] -> PartialEq[X]` x12545. I had predicted \"only the two instrumented tests fail, because cycle detection rejects the candidate anyway\". True of WI-1109's stdlib, FALSE of this one: once `Eq` forwards, the skip is what stops `Eq` joining the providers of `PartialEq`, and there is no `requires` left to be rejected against.\n * the ordering half in isolation (skip removed AND `Eq` restored to `requires`) -> 6 fail, five from the `Eq` revert and ONE from the skip: `a_weakord_goal_offers_only_carriers`. It is the only reader of that difference in the workspace, which is why `dispatch_candidate_impl_sorts` had to exist at all.\n * remove the chain entry -> 1863 fail, stdlib does not load: `missing 'requires PartialEq[T = ...]' on enclosing sort`.\n * remove the naming arm -> 46 fail: `unresolved import 'anthill.prelude.Ord.gte'` (39 sites), `'Eq.eq'` (25), `'Ord.lt'` (10), `'Ord.gt'` (8).\n\nTHE NAMING GATE HAD TO BE NARROWED, MEASURED. Wiring EVERY `provides` clause is what a first cut did, on the argument that `requires` does not ask what kind of sort it is either. `provides` is written far more often and by CARRIERS, and splicing each target's scope re-enters that target's enclosing namespace (the WI-1089/WI-993 hazard): a fixture declaring its own `Cell` beside `provides Eq[Cell]` went `ambiguous symbol 'Cell' ... [\"anthill.prelude.Cell\", \"wi1033.cell.Cell\"]` at nine sites. AND `Item::ProvidesBlock` is a DIFFERENT CLAUSE WITH THE SAME KEYWORD — `provides Int64 language rust` names the CARRIER — so wiring it spliced Int64/String/BigInt/Float into `anthill.prelude` itself: 72 load errors, every `gte`/`lt`/`max` in the tower going ambiguous. The arm now takes `ProvidesClause` only, gated on the clause binding only the sort's own parameters.\n\nDIAGNOSTICS IMPROVED, and it is the ticket's headline arriving. Comparing a `Pair[Float, ...]` now reports `unresolved: WeakOrd[T = Float]` — the condition the provision actually wrote — where it used to report `Eq[T = Float]`, one level deeper, reached ONLY by descending into `Ord`'s own requirements through the bogus candidate.\n\nSCALAND PORTED, AND IT IS NOW GREEN WHERE IT WAS NOT. Two gaps, both the same root. The loader gained the `provides`-head scope link (`processProvidesHead`); the Scala codegen gained the conversion as the `extends` clause — which closes a gap `requiresMapping`'s own note had recorded since WI-1064 (\"the is-a claim is `provides` ... NOTHING reads `ProvidesClauseItem`\") and which only became load-bearing when the towers collapsed. `trait Ord[T] extends WeakOrd[T]` and `trait Eq[T] extends PartialEq[T]` are emitted from `provides` now. Three WI-1109 expectation debts (SortedSet's slot is `WeakOrd`, not `Ord`) were also updated inline: 487/490 before, 490/490 after.\n\nTEST-EXPECTATION MOVES, all deliberate and each with its reason at the site:\n * wi857 dictionary layout: `Ord` 3 -> 1 slots. The number has now moved in two consecutive tickets, which is why it is counted there.\n * wi222 defer: proj_path [0,0] -> [0,0,0]. Ord -> WeakOrd -> Eq -> PartialEq; one more floor, same value.\n * wi869 x2: the refusal names `WeakOrd` where it named `Ord`, because that is where the requirement is now written; and the float pair names the provision's own condition.\n * wi224 x2: two fixtures pinned to a load error now load clean.\n * wi1109's header note corrected — it named `requires WeakOrd[T]` as what `an_ord_constrained_body_reaches_compare` measured.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO: `SupplySource` is READ, not merely recorded. An earlier cut set the field and consulted it nowhere while `provision_is_conversion` was recomputed at three sites; `is_conversion_edge` now reads the chain, so the predicate has ONE owner and the candidate skip cannot drift from what put the slot there.\n\nDOCS: kernel-language.md §5.1 gains \"`requires` and `provides` are BOTH chain entries\" and \"a CARRIER's `provides` and a SPEC's are two clauses with one keyword\"; the ordering-tower snippet and the `=` section are corrected. Proposal 058 §3.4's deferred-lifting note gains the rule. Library proposal 007 gains a §\"WI-1110\" closing its open questions 2 and 3 — both kept verbatim, because each was refuted by a measurement.\n\nFull workspace green (35 binaries, 2858 in wi_tests); scaland 490/490.", at: "2026-08-16T00:57:58Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1110", author: "user", content: "/code-review OUTCOME — 15 findings, and THREE of them were soundness holes I had argued myself out of. Applied, each with a driving test or a measurement.\n\nTHE INVARIANT I STATED WAS FALSE, and I had written it at two sites as though it held. I claimed `conversion => derived-through`: a row excluded from the provider search must have its carriers' rows materialized, or the exclusion DELETES an answer. But `provision_is_conversion` asked only about the CARRIER binding while `forwarded_rows_to_derive` demands IDENTITY forwarding over ALL of them, so two shapes fell in the gap — a renamed forward (`sort Ord2 { sort E = ?  provides WeakOrd[T = E] }`) and a mixed row (`provides Sp[T = A, U = Concrete]`). Both were classified conversions, both derived nothing, so `WeakOrd[C]` would have gone from one candidate to zero with the load check ALSO waived. Fixed by making identity forwarding the first conjunct, so the subset relation holds by construction rather than by assertion.\n\nThat same conjunct closes a channel I could not have closed by widening `supplies_any_operation_of`: a provision may supply the target's operations by BINDING them (`provides Monoid[T = E, combine = myCombine]`, WI-431) rather than declaring a same-named member. My operation test reads only the declaration channel, so such a witness was a \"conversion\" and would have been removed from the search entirely. An identity forwarding has no binding whose value is not a type parameter, so no op binding can hide inside one — one more instance of the recurring defect: A SUPPLY HAS MORE THAN ONE CHANNEL (WI-945).\n\nAND THE READERS ASKED A PER-PAIR QUESTION WHERE THE QUESTION IS PER-ROW. `is_conversion_edge(subject, target)` consulted the chain alone, so a sort writing BOTH `provides A[T = T]` and `provides A[T = Concrete]` had BOTH rows dropped from dispatch and BOTH provisions excused. Now the chain lookup says \"this sort has a conversion to A\" and the row's own bindings say \"and this is it\".\n\nTHE DEDUP I WROTE COULD NEVER FIRE, and the review was right about why. `direct_requires` deduped a `requires A[T]` against a `provides A[T = T]` with `views_structurally_equal`, which compares binding keys by SYMBOL — and the two clauses are loaded through different paths that key the same parameter differently (`resolve_requires_bindings` re-keys by the required spec's own param symbols). Keyed on local names now, and DRIVEN by `a_sort_writing_both_clauses_gets_one_slot`, which fails with the old key: `got [\"Low\", \"Low\"]`, two slots for one edge.\n\nA DERIVED ROW WAS BECOMING A CHAIN ENTRY. `derive_forwarded_provisions` walks a tower transitively, so `A provides B[T = T]` + `B provides C[T = T]` makes it assert `A provides C[T = T]` — a row whose shape is a conversion's. Reading it back gave `A` a second slot for a dictionary already inside its first. `derived_provision_origin_of` now gates it, which is that provenance channel's second reader.\n\nTHE COMMENT CITED A CONTROL THE TEST ITSELF DISCLAIMS. I wrote that the relocation is safe \"because a carrier that provides `Ord` but not `Eq` is refused (`an_ord_carrier_still_owes_eq`)\" — and that test's OWN doc says it measures `PartialOrd`, and forbids repairing it to `Eq`, because its carrier is a total composite `eq_derive` has already paid for. The `Eq` half had no driving test at all. `a_parametric_ord_carrier_owes_eq` now drives it on a PARAMETRIC composite, which `eq_derive` deliberately does not classify.\n\nTHE REFUSAL NAMED A CLAUSE THE AUTHOR NEVER WROTE. The whole relocation routes obligations through DERIVED rows, so `Half` — which writes only `provides Ord[T = Half]` — was told about `provides 'WeakOrd'`. That is the wording WI-1109 fixed one check over, and WI-1110 was making the un-provenanced check the PRIMARY channel and pinning the bad wording in a test. `Provision` now carries its `RuleId`, `UnsatisfiedProviderRequires` carries `derived_from`, and both message faces route through WI-1109's existing `derived_row_clause`. Asserted in the test.\n\nA CACHE THE NEXT PRODUCER OWED. I added invalidations around `derive_forwarded_provisions` and argued at the site that a producer whose rows \"happen not to matter\" must invalidate anyway — and then `eq_derive::run`, 120 lines down and also a provision producer, did not. Added; and `invalidate_requires_chain_cache`'s own doc still said its trigger was a `SortRequiresInfo` fact, which is no longer the whole rule.\n\nPERFORMANCE, measured rather than reasoned. The first cut scanned the WHOLE provision relation once per sort per chain build and took the stdlib load 0.10 s -> 0.18 s. Now: the carrier-keyed index, and the cheap identity test before the two operation-surface walks. MEASURED at parity, 0.09 s against HEAD's 0.09-0.10 s over seven warm runs each.\n\nSCALAND, three more: `processProvidesHead` swallowed an AMBIGUITY (which nobody else reports — a miss is reported by the load phase, an ambiguity is not) and omitted the `EffectsRuntime` exemption rustland carries; and `requiresMapping`'s data arms discarded `conversions` silently, against its own \"NOT A SILENT DROP, in any direction\". The data arms now emit a note saying the is-a claim is not carried and that no corpus sort writes the shape, so the mapping is unmeasured rather than assumed.\n\nONE FINDING RECORDED RATHER THAN FIXED, and it is the architectural one. The conversion stays a live `SortProvidesInfo` fact — it must, because `derive_forwarded_provisions` reads it — so readers other than the two I patched still see it: `sort_provides(Ord, WeakOrd)`, `build_sort_ops_table`, the default-provider seeding, the coherence grouping, the codegen backends. Two `continue`s is exactly the shape WI-1109's own note warns about (\"patching two readers surfaced a third\"). MEASURED that none of them answers wrongly today, and the reason is uniform: nothing has type `Ord` or `Eq`, so a reader treating the conversion as a provision reaches a carrier that never arrives. THE FINISHED SHAPE MARKS THE ROW — as `mark_derived_provision` marks a derived one — so the relation itself distinguishes a conversion and no reader needs teaching. That is a change to the provision relation's schema, and this ticket already moves the dictionary layout twice; stated at the skip site so whoever needs it finds the argument rather than rediscovering it.\n\nRe-verified after every fix: full workspace 35/35 binaries, 0 failures (2860 in wi_tests); scaland 490/490; stdlib load at parity.", at: "2026-08-16T01:25:57Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1110", author: "user", content: "ACCEPTANCE CLOSED OUT 2026-08-16 — three bullets were still undriven when this was marked delivered, and driving them found one thing worth recording.\n\n * 'a body dispatching a WeakOrd spec op with NO requires in scope is still REFUSED, with the control that the same body WITH the requires loads and runs' — now `a_weakord_dispatch_with_no_requires_is_refused` + `the_same_weakord_dispatch_with_a_requires_runs`. This is the VACUITY guard the whole exclusion rests on: the Eq experiment showed how it fails, a universal provider row makes the goal resolve and the refusal disappear, so the program loads and dies at eval.\n * 'the Half fixture reports no impl provides WeakOrd rather than a cycle' — now `a_carrier_with_no_compare_is_told_nothing_provides_weakord`, asserting BOTH the truthful sentence and the absence of `construction is cyclic`.\n\nAND THE SHAPE MATTERED, WHICH I DID NOT EXPECT. My first fixture called `WeakOrd.compare(Half.half(9), Half.half(4))` DIRECTLY from a sort with no requires, and it LOADS CLEAN. Probed three shapes:\n\n  A. direct concrete-carrier call, no requires  -> CLEAN LOAD\n  B. abstract T with `requires PartialOrd[T]`  -> 'missing requires WeakOrd[T = ...] on enclosing sort'\n  C. concrete carrier through a holder that DOES declare the requirement\n                                                -> 'no impl provides anthill.prelude.WeakOrd; Half provides no\n                                                    anthill.prelude.WeakOrd — declare provides ...' and NO cycle\n\nA is not this ticket's doing and not a regression: it is the mask `ordered.anthill` already documents at its op-scoped `requires WeakOrd[T]` — `check_one_spec_op_requirement` returns early for a builtin functor, so the CALL-SITE half of a spec-op requirement goes unchecked while the op is still a resolver builtin. WI-876 measured the same for `PartialOrd.gt` ('a generic operation with requires PartialOrd[T], and even one with no requires at all, calls PartialOrd.gt(a, b) and loads clean'), and WI-879 is where it comes due. A fixture in shape A measures the mask, not the exclusion — recorded at the test's site so the next reader does not pick it again.\n\nC is the acceptance, and it passes. Full workspace 35/35 binaries, 0 failures.", at: "2026-08-16T06:17:06Z")
